Let  $s_i$  be the number of questions the  $i^{\text{th}}$  person solved, and  $n$  be the total number of problems on the test. Thus, we have that there are  $s_i(n - s_i)$  number of unordered sets of 2 problems such that the person solves only one of them. Thus, by AM-GM,
\begin{align*}
  \sum s_i(n - s_i) & \leq \frac{11n^2}4
\end{align*}
However, by given, we also have the  $LHS \geq 6\binom n2.$  Thus, by solving  $6\binom n2 \leq \frac{11n^2}4,$  we have that  $n\leq12.$

Let  $a_i$  denote the students, and let  $x_i$  be the number of problems they solve correctly. Also let  $n$  be the total number of problems. Hence, we can compute that the total number of pairs of problems containing one problem that a student solved and another that they missed is  $\sum_{i} x_i (n-x_i)$ . Dividing over the total number of pairs, we expect this to be equal to  $6$ :

\[
\sum_{i} x_i (n-x_i) = 6{n\choose 2}
\]

Next, observe that by Jensen and the concavity of the function  $f(x_i) = x_i (n-x_i)$ , we can construct an inequality on the left hand side: 

\begin{align*}
    6{n\choose 2} = \sum x_i (n-x_i) &\leq 11 f\left(\frac{x_1 + x_2 \cdots x_n}{11}\right) 
    &\leq 11\left(\frac{n^2}{4}\right) 
    \Rightarrow \frac{11}{4} n^2 &\geq 6{n\choose 2} 
    \Rightarrow n \leq 12
\end{align*}

as desired. 

