<details><summary>Click to expand</summary>Let  $a$ ,  $b$ ,  $c$  be positive reals such that  $ab+bc+ca=1$ .
Substitution  $x=bc$ ,  $y=ca$ ,  $z=ab$  yields expressions  $a=\sqrt{\dfrac{yz}{x}}$ ,  $b=\sqrt{\dfrac{zx}{y}}$ ,  $c=\sqrt{\dfrac{xy}{z}}$  where   $0< x,y,z$ ,  $x+y+z =1$ .
Therefore
        \begin{align*}
            &\frac{a+b}{\sqrt{ab(1-ab)}} + \frac{b+c}{\sqrt{bc(1-bc)}} + \frac{c+a}{\sqrt{ca(1-ca)}}  
            =& \sum \frac{\sqrt{\frac{yz}{x}}+\sqrt{\frac{xz}{y}}}{\sqrt{z(1-z)}} = \sum \frac{x+y}{\sqrt{x+y}\sqrt{xy}}
        \end{align*}
        \[
            \frac{\sqrt{2}}{abc} = \frac{\sqrt{2}}{\sqrt{xyz}}
        \]
Hence we have some equivalents;
        \begin{align*}
            & \frac{a+b}{\sqrt{ab(1-ab)}} + \frac{b+c}{\sqrt{bc(1-bc)}} + \frac{c+a}{\sqrt{ca(1-ca)}} \le \frac{\sqrt{2}}{abc}
            \iff & \sum \frac{x+y}{\sqrt{x+y}\sqrt{xy}} \le \frac{\sqrt{2}}{\sqrt{xyz}}
        \end{align*}
        \[
            \frac{x+y}{\sqrt{x+y}\sqrt{xy}} \le \frac{\sqrt{2}}{\sqrt{xyz}} \iff \sum \sqrt{(1-z)z} \le \sqrt{2}
        \]
By the way, analyzing the function  $z \mapsto \sqrt{(1-z)z}$  at  $z=\frac{1}{3}$ , one can expect the inequality  $\sqrt{(1-z)z} \le \frac{\sqrt{2}}{4} + \frac{z}{2\sqrt{2}}$  which is equivalent to the trivial inequality  $ -\frac{1}{8} (3 z-1)^2 \le 0$  and hence is true.
Therefore,
        \[
            \sum \sqrt{(1-z)z} \le \sum \left(\frac{\sqrt{2}}{4} + \frac{z}{2\sqrt{2}}\right) = \sqrt{2}
        \]
Equality holds when and only when  $x=y=z=\frac{1}{3}$ , i.e.  $a=b=c=\frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}$ .</details>
