Instead we show that the intersection of  $MD$  and  $BO,$  which we will call  $P',$  lies on  $(MAO).$  The central claim is that  $PABD$  is cyclic.

Note  $\angle PDA=\angle MDA=90^{\circ}-\angle C,$  and also note that  $\angle PAD=\angle PAB-\angle DAB.$  Note that  $\angle PAB=\angle C$  since  $\angle APB=90^{\circ}$  and  $\angle ABP=\angle ABO=90^{\circ}-\angle C$  and  $\angle BAD=90^{\circ}-\angle B.$  Thus  $\angle PAD=\angle B+\angle C-90^{\circ}.$ Now consider  $\triangle PAD.$  Note  $\angle DPA=180^{\circ}-(\angle PDA+\angle PAD)=180^{\circ}-\angle B.$  Thus  $PABD$  is cyclic.

This implies that  $\angle APO=\angle APB=\angle ADB=90^{\circ}.$  Since  $\angle AMO=90^{\circ}$  as well, we are done.

