The problem is still true for any arbitrary line  $\ell$ . So, we remove the orthocenter  $H$ .
Define the point  $T$  to be an intersection of  $\ell_1$  and  $\ell_2$ , and  $K$  be an intersection of  $\overline{AR}$  and  $\overline{BC}$ .
[rule]**<span style="color:#f00">Claim 1.</span>**  $\triangle{B'A'T}\sim\triangle{BKR}$ .
*Proof.* We prove  $\measuredangle{B'TA'}=\measuredangle{BRK}$  by  $$ \measuredangle{B'TA'}=\measuredangle(\ell_2,\ell_1)=\measuredangle{ACB}=\measuredangle{ARB}=\measuredangle{KRB}, $$  and prove  $\measuredangle{B'A'T}=\measuredangle{RKB}$  by  $$ \measuredangle{B'A'T}=\measuredangle{BA'A}+\measuredangle{TA'A}=\measuredangle(KB,\ell_1)+\measuredangle(\ell_1,RK)=\measuredangle{RKB}. $$  These imply  $\triangle{B'A'T\sim \triangle{BKR}}$ .
[rule]
<span style="color:#f00">**Claim 2.**</span>  $\triangle{PB'T}\sim \triangle{PBR}$ .
*Proof.* We already have  $\measuredangle{PB'T}=\measuredangle{RBP}$  from claim 1. By ratio spamming on  $\triangle{PBB'}\sim PA'K$  and  $\triangle{B'A'T}\sim\triangle{KRB}$  we have:  $$ \frac{PB'}{PB}=\frac{B'A'}{BK}=\frac{B'T}{BR}. $$  It immediately yields the desired result.
[rule]
It is easy to see that  $\measuredangle{B'PB}=\measuredangle{QPR}$  because  $\measuredangle(\ell,PQ)=\measuredangle(PT,\ell)$ , and  $$ \frac{PB}{PB'}=\frac{PT}{PR}=\frac{PQ}{PR}. $$  Therefore,  $\triangle{PB'B}\sim \triangle{PQR}$ . In conclusion  $\angle{PQR}=90^{\circ}$  as desired.

