This is a problem about [url = https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vandermonde_matrix]Vandermonde determinants[/url].

Observe that by definition,   $$ A = 
\begin{bmatrix} 
1 && \dbinom{n-1}{1} \alpha _1 && \dbinom{n-1}{2} \alpha _1 ^2 && \dots && \dbinom{n-1}{n-1} \alpha _1 ^{n-1} 

1 && \dbinom{n-1}{1} \alpha _2 && \dbinom{n-1}{2} \alpha_2^2 && \dots && \dbinom{n-1}{n-1} \alpha _2^ {n-1}  
\vdots && && && \ddots && \vdots
1 && \dbinom{n-1}{1} \alpha_n && \dbinom{n-1}{2} \alpha_n ^2 && \dots && \dbinom{n-1}{n-1} \alpha _n ^{n-1}
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix} 
1 && 1 && \dots && 1 
\beta _1 && \beta_2 && \dots && \beta _n 
\beta_1^2 && \beta_2^2 && \dots && \beta _n^2  
\vdots && && \ddots && \vdots
\beta_1^{n-1} && \beta_2^{n-1} && \dots && \beta _n^{n-1} 
\end{bmatrix} $$  (I promise that they're both  $n \times n$  matrices!)

Thus,   $$ \det (A) = \dbinom{n-1}{1} \dbinom{n-1}{2} \cdots \dbinom{n-1}{n-1} \det \left( \begin{bmatrix} 
1 && \alpha _1 &&\alpha _1 ^2 && \dots && \alpha _1 ^{n-1} 

1 && \alpha _2 &&  \alpha_2^2 && \dots &&  \alpha _2^ {n-1}  
\vdots && && && \ddots && \vdots
1 && \alpha_n && \alpha_n ^2 && \dots &&  \alpha _n ^{n-1}
\end{bmatrix} \right) \det \left(\begin{bmatrix} 
1 && 1 && \dots && 1 
\beta _1 && \beta_2 && \dots && \beta _n 
\beta_1^2 && \beta_2^2 && \dots && \beta _n^2  
\vdots && && \ddots && \vdots
\beta_1^{n-1} && \beta_2^{n-1} && \dots && \beta _n^{n-1} 
\end{bmatrix} \right) $$  Notice that although the two matrices are pretty different, they are both Vandermonde matrices themselves. The determinant is computed identically for both of them: think about cofactor expansions along either the row or column containing only  $1$ 's. In fact, by the way the sequences of  $\alpha_i$  and  $\beta _j$  have been defined, they will be equal, as we shall see in the following calculation! Now you can either think through this next step yourself, or keep the following convenient fact memorized: the determinant of a Vandermonde matrix is given by   $$ \pm \prod _{1 \leq i < j \leq n} (a_j - a_i). $$  The  $\pm$  is there to represent the fact that the determinant is sensitive to the number of  $\textbf{odd permutations}$  of parameters (recall that the difference between an even and odd permutation is the number of transpositions required to (uniquely) express the given permutation). Keeping this in mind, we obtain that   $$ \det (A) = \pm \frac{(n-1)!^n}{(1! 2!\cdots (n-1)!)^2} \cdot (1! 2!\cdots (n-1)!)^2 = \boxed{\pm (n-1)!^n}. $$  In case there is any confusion about the "extra" power of  $(n-1)!$  in the numerator, we multiply both numerator and denominator by a factor of  $(n-1)!$  as to cancel out the denominator and the evaluated product across a given permutation of the set of the first  $n$  natural numbers.

