It's easy to show that   $$ \lim_{n\to\infty} S_n(a) = \begin{cases} 0 & a<2019  \infty & a>2019 \end{cases} $$  without the argument I will show. But it's difficult to show that  $\{S_n(2019)\}$  converges to a positive real. Following is the official solution.

It suffices to show that  $$ \lim_{n\to\infty} n^s \sum_{k=1}^{n-1} \frac{1}{k^s (n-k)^s} = 2\zeta(s),\quad s>1 $$  Choose some  $\alpha\in \left(\frac{1}{s},1\right)$ . Define  $a_n,b_n,c_n$  as
\begin{align*}
a_n &= n^s \sum_{k=1}^{n^\alpha} \frac{1}{k^s (n-k)^s} 
b_n &= n^s \sum_{k=n^\alpha+1}^{n-n^\alpha-1} \frac{1}{k^s (n-k)^s}
\end{align*}
First, we have  $$  \sum_{k=1}^{n^\alpha} \frac{1}{k^s} \leq a_n \leq \frac{n^s}{(n-n^\alpha)^s} \sum_{k=1}^{n^\alpha} \frac{1}{k^s} $$  Hence,  $\lim\limits_{n\to\infty} a_n = \zeta(s)$ .
Next, for sufficiently large  $n$  and  $n^\alpha<k<n-n^\alpha$ , we have  $$ k^s (n-k)^s > (n^\alpha)^s (n-n^\alpha)^s > (n^\alpha)^s (\frac{1}{2} n)^s = \frac{1}{2^s} n^{s(\alpha+1)} $$  so  $$ b_n \leq n^s (n-2 n^\alpha) \cdot \frac{2^s}{ n^{s(\alpha+1)}} < \frac{2^s}{n^{s\alpha-1}} \to 0 \text{ as }n\to\infty $$  Lastly, note that  $$ a_n = n^s \sum_{k=n-n^\alpha}^{n-1} \frac{1}{k^s (n-k)^s} $$  Therefore,   $$ \lim_{n\to\infty} n^s \sum_{k=1}^{n-1} \frac{1}{k^s (n-k)^s} = 2\lim_{n\to\infty} a_n +  \lim_{n\to\infty} b_n = 2\zeta(s) $$

