Repeated substitution gives an equation in  $x_1^2$  only and this equation involves a continued fraction,  $x_1^2=[4;-1,4,-1,4,\cdots,-1,x_1^2]$  where the continued fraction has  $2n+1$  terms. We can evaluate this using the standard method for simple continued fractions.

\begin{tabular}{cc|ccccccccc}
&&4&-1&4&-1&4&-1&4& $\cdots$ \hline
0&1&4&-3&-8&5&12&-7&-16& $\cdots$ 1&0&1&-1&-3&2&5&-3&-7& $\cdots$ \end{tabular}

An easy induction proof shows that for each  $1\leq k\leq n$ , the  $(2k-1)$ th convergent has numerator  $(-1)^{k+1}\cdot 4k$  and denominator  $(-1)^{k+1}(2k-1)$  while the  $2k$ th convergent has numerator  $(-1)^k(2k+1)$  and denominator  $(-1)^k\cdot k$ .

So this means:
\begin{align*}
x_1^2=\frac{(-1)^n(2n+1)x_1^2+(-1)^{n+1}\cdot 4n}{(-1)^n\cdot nx_1^2+(-1)^{n+1}(2n-1)}=\frac{(2n+1)x_1^2-4n}{nx_1^2-(2n-1)}
&\implies nx_1^4-(2n-1)x_1^2=(2n+1)x_1^2-4n
&\implies nx_1^4-4nx_1^2+4n=0
&\implies n(x_1^2-2)^2=0
&\implies x_1^2=2
\end{align*}

Then we get  $x_2^2=\frac{1}{4-x_1^2}=\frac{1}{4-2}=\frac{1}{2}$ ,  $x_3^2=\frac{1}{1-x_2^2}=\frac{1}{1-\frac{1}{2}}=2$ , etc.

That is,  $x_{2k-1}^2=2$  and  $x_{2k}^2=\frac{1}{2}$  for all  $1\leq k\leq n$ .

So the solution set is all  $2n$ -tuples  $\left(\pm\sqrt{2},\pm\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2},\pm\sqrt{2},\pm\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2},\cdots,\pm\sqrt{2},\pm\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right)$  for every combination of the signs. This is a total of  $4^n$  different solutions.

